%[text] # comp.Enclosure Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Base class for the enclosure of a loudspeaker driver.
%[text] **Namespace:** comp
%[text] **Superclasses:** none
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] `comp.Enclosure` is the abstract base class of the enclosures in the `comp` namespace. An enclosure holds the driver in `Driver` and describes the acoustical side of the loudspeaker system: everything at the front and at the rear of the diaphragm, from the diaphragm to the ambient pressure. `lspsys` uses the enclosure in its network, see [solve2PortNetwork](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))). The symbols are listed in [Symbols](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','symbols.m'))).
%[text] The subclasses are [`comp.ClosedBox`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','closedbox','closedboxdoc.m'))), `comp.BassReflex`, `comp.FrontLoadedHorn` and `comp.FeaEnclosure`; only `comp.ClosedBox` is complete.
%%
%[text] ### The two 4-ports of an enclosure
%[text] Every enclosure describes its acoustical side with two 4-ports on three conductors: the front of the diaphragm (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear of the diaphragm (bottom). $ T\_{a,e} $ (`tae`) holds the enclosure and $ T\_{a,rad} $ (`tarad`) the radiation into the ambient pressure. Together they form the two-port network $ T\_a $ of the acoustical side:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:e4c1)
%[text] A subclass implements `tae` and `tarad`; it is free to put any element at the front, at the rear, to the ambient pressure or between front and rear, which covers every linear enclosure and radiation. The 4-ports lead all volume velocity to the ambient pressure, so port 2 of $ T\_a $ stays open. `lspsys.solve2PortNetwork` reduces the two 4-ports to $ T\_a $ and derives the radiated volume velocity $ U\_{rad} $ from them. The flows count to the right on the front conductor and to the left on the rear conductor.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] `comp.Enclosure` is abstract: create an enclosure with the constructor of a subclass, for example `comp.ClosedBox`. The constructor of `comp.Enclosure` gives the enclosure a default driver, `comp.Driver`, with all its parameters set to zero.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%%
%[text] ### `Driver` — Driver in the enclosure
%[text] `(1,1) comp.Driver`
%[text] Driver that is mounted in the enclosure. Default: `comp.Driver`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text:table]
%[text] | Method | Type | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `tae` | Abstract | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ T\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure, for frequencies `f` and radiation angle `ra`, as a 4 × 4 × `numel(f)` array. |
%[text] | `tarad` | Abstract | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ T\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation, for frequencies `f` and radiation angle `ra`, as a 4 × 4 × `numel(f)` array. |
%[text] | `zarad` | Static | Radiation impedance $ Z\_{rad} $ in Pa.s/m3 of a rigid circular piston with radius `rd` in an infinite baffle, at the angular frequencies `w`. |
%[text] | `struve` | Static | Approximation of the Struve function $ H\_1 $ of the first kind (Aarts and Janssen), used by `zarad`. |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Radiation impedance of a piston
%[text] Plot the real and the imaginary part of the radiation impedance of a piston with a radius of 0.1 m, normalized to $ \\rho c / (\\pi r\_d^2) $, against $ k r\_d $. At low frequency the imaginary part (the air load mass) dominates; at high frequency the real part tends to 1.
rd = 0.1;
f = logspace(1,4,500);
w = 2*pi*f;
Zrad = comp.Enclosure.zarad(rd,w);
normalized = Zrad/(lspsys.AirDensity*lspsys.SpeedOfSound/(pi*rd^2));
ka = w/lspsys.SpeedOfSound*rd;
semilogx(ka,real(normalized),ka,imag(normalized),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:2d7ce940]
grid on %[output:2d7ce940]
xlabel("k r_d") %[output:2d7ce940]
ylabel("Normalized radiation impedance") %[output:2d7ce940]
legend("Real part","Imaginary part",Location="northwest") %[output:2d7ce940]
title("Radiation impedance of a piston in an infinite baffle") %[output:2d7ce940]

